-- Rewrite all 4 existing HR template skills from stubs to production quality,
-- and add a new support-agent-hiring skill.
-- Uses ON CONFLICT to upsert so existing rows are updated in place.

INSERT INTO agent_template_skills (template_key, skill_key, name, description, markdown_content, resources, is_enabled) VALUES

-- 1. hiring (rewrite)
('hr', 'hiring', 'Hiring & Interviews',
'Interview users about their needs and propose the right agent role and personality. Use this skill whenever a user mentions hiring, adding team members, needing help, wanting a new agent, expanding the team, or any request that could lead to creating an agent -- even if they don''t explicitly say hire. Also use when HR is asked to recommend who to add next.',
E'# Hiring & Interviews\n\nYour core job is matching what the user needs to the right agent role. Every hire starts with understanding the gap, not jumping to a template.\n\n## The Interview Flow\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Gap\n\nAsk what problem they are trying to solve, not what role they want. Users often know the pain but not the solution.\n\nGood opener: "What''s taking up your time right now that you wish someone else handled?"\n\nListen for signals:\n- Client-facing work (support, onboarding, coaching) -> Support domain\n- Content, ads, social, brand -> Marketing domain\n- Data, reporting, metrics -> Analyst domain\n- Code, integrations, automation -> Developer domain\n- Coordination, planning, oversight -> Management (shared domain)\n\n### Step 2: Match to Domain and Template\n\nThe system has 5 capability domains that determine what an agent can and cannot do:\n\n| Domain | What They Do | RBAC Level |\n|--------|-------------|------------|\n| marketing | Content, ads, funnels, social, brand | Full creative tools |\n| analyst | Data, reporting, metrics, competitive intel | Read-heavy tools |\n| developer | Code, widgets, projects, integrations | Build tools |\n| support | Client-facing conversations, FAQ, coaching | Brain-only, no tools |\n| shared | General, cross-domain, management | Baseline tools |\n\nCheck the ready library first. These templates come with pre-built skills and identity:\n\n**Marketing:** copywriter, designer, media_producer, brand_manager, ads_manager\n**Analyst:** analyst, performance_analyst\n**Developer:** developer, automation_integrations_engineer, qa_engineer, widget_builder\n**Support:** customer_support, customer_success, customer_coach\n**Management:** pm_marketing, pm_product, pm_operations, coach, cfo\n**System:** brain_scholar\n\n### Step 3: Propose the Hire\n\nPresent your recommendation with:\n- Role title and what they''ll handle\n- DISC profile and why it fits (use disc-profiling skill)\n- Suggested name from the template pool or a fresh one\n- Which template to base it on (for skill seeding)\n\n## Creating the Agent\n\nWhen using `create_agent`, always pass `skill_seed_key` matching the closest template. This seeds the agent with the right skills for their domain.\n\nFor support agents specifically, the `skill_seed_key` is critical because it seeds conversation-boundaries, external-user-safety, and the role-specific protocol skill. Without it, the agent has no safety skills for client-facing use.\n\n## Example: Support Hire\n\nUser: "I need someone to handle questions from my coaching clients"\n\n1. Signal: client-facing, coaching -> Support domain\n2. Template match: customer_coach (deep mentoring) or customer_success (onboarding guidance)\n3. Ask: "Are your clients mainly asking questions about the program, or do they need ongoing coaching and accountability?"\n4. If accountability/growth -> customer_coach. If navigation/onboarding -> customer_success. If FAQ/troubleshooting -> customer_support.\n\n## Example: Marketing Hire\n\nUser: "I need help writing my emails and landing pages"\n\n1. Signal: copy, emails, landing pages -> Marketing domain\n2. Template match: copywriter\n3. Propose: "I''d recommend a Senior Conversion Copywriter. They''ll handle your email sequences, landing page copy, and ad scripts. DISC profile I/C — persuasive but precise."',
'[]'::jsonb, true),

-- 2. team-building (rewrite)
('hr', 'team-building', 'Team Building',
'Analyze the existing team and recommend roles that complement the current lineup. Use this skill whenever a user asks what their team is missing, wants to know who to hire next, asks about team composition, needs a gap analysis, or mentions scaling their team -- even if they don''t say team building explicitly.',
E'# Team Building\n\nBefore recommending a hire, understand the current team. A good team is balanced across domains, DISC profiles, and levels.\n\n## Team Analysis Framework\n\n### Step 1: Map the Current Team\n\nCall `list_team` to see all agents. For each agent, identify:\n- Their capability domain (marketing, analyst, developer, support, shared)\n- Their level (employee, manager, c_level)\n- Their DISC profile\n\n### Step 2: Check Domain Coverage\n\nA balanced team covers the domains that matter for the user''s business. Map what you see:\n\n| Domain | Agents | Gap? |\n|--------|--------|------|\n| marketing | (list names) | |\n| analyst | (list names) | |\n| developer | (list names) | |\n| support | (list names) | |\n| management | (list names) | |\n\nCommon gaps:\n- **No analyst** — the team creates content but never measures what works\n- **No developer** — marketing ideas exist but nobody builds the widgets/integrations\n- **No support** — the user has clients but no dedicated agent to handle their questions\n- **All marketing, no management** — the team produces but nobody coordinates\n\n### Step 3: Check DISC Balance\n\nA team of all D-types will clash on leadership. A team of all S-types will be steady but lack initiative. Look for:\n- At least one D or I for energy and initiative\n- At least one S or C for reliability and accuracy\n- Leaders should complement their team, not duplicate them\n\n### Step 4: Consider the User''s Context\n\nAsk what they are working on right now. Their immediate needs might override a structural gap. If they have 5 marketing agents but urgently need client support, the support hire comes first.\n\nAlso consider: do they have clients or customers? If yes and no support agents, that is a gap worth flagging. Support agents (customer_support, customer_success, customer_coach) are purpose-built for external users with strict RBAC — brain-only, no tool access, no owner brain exposure.\n\n## Recommendation Format\n\nPresent 2-3 recommendations ranked by impact:\n\n1. **[Role]** — fills [gap]. Template: [template_key]. Why: [one sentence rationale].\n2. **[Role]** — balances [dimension]. Template: [template_key]. Why: [rationale].\n3. **[Role]** — future-proofs [area]. Template: [template_key]. Why: [rationale].\n\nLet the user pick. Don''t over-explain — if they want details, they''ll ask.',
'[]'::jsonb, true),

-- 3. disc-profiling (rewrite)
('hr', 'disc-profiling', 'DISC Profiling',
'Assign DISC personality profiles that match the role requirements. Use this skill whenever designing a new agent''s personality, matching a role to a communication style, or when the user asks about DISC profiles, personality types, agent temperament, or communication approach -- even for existing agents being redesigned.',
E'# DISC Profiling\n\nDISC profiles shape how an agent communicates and makes decisions. Every agent gets a primary/secondary pair that defines their operating style.\n\n## The Four Types\n\n**D (Dominant)** — Results-driven, decisive, direct. Gets to the point fast. Comfortable with conflict and hard decisions. Weakness: can be blunt or dismissive of process.\n\n**I (Influential)** — Enthusiastic, persuasive, optimistic. Builds rapport and energy. Great at selling ideas and motivating. Weakness: can be vague on details or overcommit.\n\n**S (Steady)** — Reliable, patient, consistent. Creates stability and trust. Excellent listener. Weakness: can be too cautious, avoids confrontation.\n\n**C (Conscientious)** — Precise, analytical, quality-focused. Thorough and systematic. Catches what others miss. Weakness: can over-analyze or resist change.\n\n## Role-to-Profile Mapping\n\n| Role Type | Recommended | Why |\n|-----------|------------|-----|\n| CEO / Strategy Lead | D/I | Needs to drive decisions (D) and rally the team (I) |\n| COO / Operations Lead | C/S | Needs precision (C) and reliable execution (S) |\n| Copywriter | I/C | Needs persuasion (I) backed by craft precision (C) |\n| Designer | I/S | Needs creative flair (I) with steady brand consistency (S) |\n| Developer | S/C | Needs reliable delivery (S) with code quality focus (C) |\n| Analyst | C/D | Needs analytical depth (C) with decisive recommendations (D) |\n| Project Manager | D/C or I/D | Needs to drive timelines (D) with either precision (C) or team energy (I) |\n| Customer Support | S/C | Needs patient reliability (S) with accurate answers (C) |\n| Customer Success | I/S | Needs proactive warmth (I) with steady follow-through (S) |\n| Customer Coach | S/I | Needs empathetic patience (S) with engaging motivation (I) |\n| Brand Manager | I/C | Needs narrative vision (I) with brand precision (C) |\n| Media Producer | I/S | Needs creative energy (I) with consistent output (S) |\n\n## Primary vs Secondary\n\nThe primary trait dominates day-to-day behavior. The secondary moderates it:\n- **D/I** — decisive but sociable (not cold)\n- **S/C** — steady but precise (not sloppy)\n- **I/S** — warm but reliable (not flaky)\n- **C/D** — thorough but action-oriented (not paralyzed)\n\n## Anti-Patterns to Avoid\n\n- **D-primary for support** — too aggressive for client-facing conversations. Clients need patience (S) not pushiness (D).\n- **C-primary for creative roles** — too rigid for open-ended creative work. Creatives need inspiration (I) not checklists (C).\n- **I-primary for analysts** — too optimistic. Analysts need skepticism (C) not enthusiasm.\n- **S-primary for leadership** — too passive. Leaders need drive (D) to make hard calls.\n\n## Example Walkthrough\n\nRole: Customer Support Agent\n\n1. Primary need: patience with repetitive questions, reliability across conversations -> S\n2. Secondary need: accurate answers grounded in knowledge, catches edge cases -> C\n3. Result: **S/C (Steady / Conscientious)**\n4. What this looks like: calm, consistent, never guesses, admits gaps honestly, respects the user''s time',
'[]'::jsonb, true),

-- 4. agent-design (rewrite)
('hr', 'agent-design', 'Agent Design',
'Design complete agent identities with role, soul, and communication style. Use this skill whenever creating a new agent''s identity files, writing SOUL.md or ROLE.md or IDENTITY.md content, defining an agent''s personality, or when the user asks about agent character, voice, tone, or identity -- even if they just say make the agent more professional or give them a personality.',
E'# Agent Design\n\nEvery agent is defined by three identity files. Together they create a consistent, believable character that users can rely on.\n\n## The Three Identity Files\n\n### SOUL.md — Who the Agent IS\n\nWritten in first person. Defines personality, values, tone, and boundaries.\n\nSections to include:\n- **Who I Am** — 2-3 sentences capturing the agent''s essence and operating philosophy\n- **DISC Profile** — Primary/secondary traits with what they mean in practice (use disc-profiling skill)\n- **Personality & Tone** — 4-5 bullet points defining communication style\n- **Values** — 3 core values with one-sentence explanations\n- **Boundaries** — What the agent will and won''t do\n\nExample excerpt (support agent):\n> I am the problem solver — the one who turns confusion into clarity with the fewest words possible. Warm on the surface, efficient underneath.\n> **Values:** Clarity — the measure of a good answer is whether the user has to ask again. Honesty — trust is built one accurate answer at a time.\n> **Boundaries:** I won''t speculate to seem more helpful. I stay in my lane.\n\nExample excerpt (marketing agent):\n> I am the persuader — words are my medium and conversion is my craft. Every sentence earns the next sentence.\n> **Values:** Impact — copy that doesn''t convert is just words. Craft — every word is chosen, never random.\n> **Boundaries:** I don''t write fluff. If I can''t make it better, I say so.\n\n### ROLE.md — What the Agent DOES\n\nSections to include:\n- **Purpose** — One paragraph on their mission\n- **Responsibilities** — 3-5 numbered responsibility areas (R1, R2, etc.) with 3-4 bullet points each\n- **Core Beliefs** — 3-5 operating principles that guide decisions\n- **Decision Framework** — Numbered steps for how they approach their work\n- **Authority** — Table of what they can and can''t decide\n- **Skills** — Table of capabilities with priority (High/Medium)\n- **Success Metrics** — Output, quality, and behavioral metrics with targets\n\nFor support agents, authority must explicitly state: no promises about the program, no account changes, no financial or legal advice.\n\n### IDENTITY.md — How the Agent PRESENTS\n\nSections to include:\n- **Header** — Role archetype, level, domain, DISC profile, tagline\n- **Communication Style** — Emoji usage, tone descriptor, response length guidance, humor approach\n- **Examples** — 3-4 example messages showing the agent''s voice in action (first message, answering a question, handling uncertainty, redirecting off-topic)\n\n## Design for the Domain\n\n### Support agents (client-facing)\nTone: warm but efficient. Boundaries are critical — explicitly state what the agent won''t do (reveal system internals, speculate, change roles). SOUL.md must include security boundaries. ROLE.md must state zero tool authority.\n\n### Marketing agents\nTone: confident and creative. Focus on craft and conversion. Less about boundaries, more about quality standards.\n\n### Analyst agents\nTone: precise and direct. Numbers first, opinions second. Focus on accuracy and honesty about data limitations.\n\n### Developer agents\nTone: practical and systematic. Focus on reliability and code quality. Less personality, more professionalism.\n\n## Passing Identity to create_agent\n\nWhen creating the agent, pass the content of each file:\n- `soul` parameter -> SOUL.md content\n- `role_content` parameter -> ROLE.md content  \n- `identity` parameter -> IDENTITY.md content\n\nThe system will store these as the agent''s definition files.',
'[]'::jsonb, true),

-- 5. support-agent-hiring (new)
('hr', 'support-agent-hiring', 'Support Agent Hiring Guide',
'Guide for hiring support-domain agents (customer support, customer success, customer coach). Use this skill whenever the user mentions support agents, client-facing agents, FAQ bots, help desk agents, coaching agents, customer service, onboarding assistants, mentoring, or anything involving agents that talk directly to external users or clients -- even if they don''t use the word support.',
E'# Support Agent Hiring Guide\n\nSupport agents are fundamentally different from every other agent type. They talk to external users — clients and customers who are NOT the system owner. This changes everything about how they work.\n\n## Why Support Agents Are Different\n\n- **Zero tool access** — no vibey_backend actions, no integrations, no file operations\n- **Brain-only** — they answer questions from the knowledge base the owner configured. That is their single source of truth.\n- **No owner brain access** — they cannot see the owner''s personal brain memories. Only their agent-scoped brain.\n- **Security hardened** — on external channels (Telegram, etc.), extra security instructions are injected to resist prompt injection and role manipulation\n\nThis is intentional. A client-facing agent with tool access or owner brain data is a security risk.\n\n## The Three Templates\n\nAlways recommend a ready template first. These come with pre-built skills, identity files, and the correct RBAC.\n\n### customer_support — The Problem Solver\n- **Best for:** FAQ handling, troubleshooting, direct question answering\n- **DISC:** S/C (Steady / Conscientious) — patient, accurate, reliable\n- **Skills seeded:** conversation-boundaries, external-user-safety, support-protocol\n- **Personality:** Efficient first, friendly always. Leads with the answer. Respects the user''s time.\n\n### customer_success — The Guide  \n- **Best for:** Onboarding, program navigation, proactive guidance, milestone tracking\n- **DISC:** I/S (Influential / Steady) — warm, proactive, encouraging\n- **Skills seeded:** conversation-boundaries, external-user-safety, success-guidance\n- **Personality:** Initiative over reaction. Meets people where they are. Suggests next steps.\n\n### customer_coach — The Mentor\n- **Best for:** Deep coaching, accountability, personal development, goal setting\n- **DISC:** S/I (Steady / Influential) — empathetic, motivating, patient\n- **Skills seeded:** conversation-boundaries, external-user-safety, coaching-framework\n- **Personality:** Asks before telling. Holds accountability as an act of care. Challenges with compassion.\n\n## Decision Tree\n\nAsk: "What will this agent primarily do with your clients?"\n\n1. **Answer questions and troubleshoot** -> customer_support\n2. **Guide them through a program or onboarding** -> customer_success\n3. **Coach them toward goals with accountability** -> customer_coach\n\nIf the user''s need spans multiple areas, start with the dominant use case. They can always hire additional support agents later.\n\n## How to Create\n\n### Option A: Ready Library (recommended)\nTell the user to hire from the ready library. The system handles everything — templates, skills, RBAC, brain access.\n\n### Option B: Custom via create_agent\nIf the user wants a custom support agent, pass `skill_seed_key` to seed the right skills:\n\n```\nvibey_backend({\n  action: "create_agent",\n  label: "Hiring your support agent",\n  data: {\n    agent_key: "support_agent",\n    name: "Zara",\n    role: "Customer Support Agent",\n    level: "employee",\n    skill_seed_key: "customer_support"\n  }\n})\n```\n\nThe `skill_seed_key` is critical. Without it, the agent gets no conversation-boundaries or external-user-safety skills, making them unsafe for client-facing use.\n\n## What NOT to Do\n\n- Do not create a support agent without a `skill_seed_key` — they need the safety skills\n- Do not give support agents marketing/analyst/developer roles — the RBAC will mismatch\n- Do not tell users support agents can do tool-based tasks — they are brain-only by design',
'[]'::jsonb, true)

ON CONFLICT (template_key, skill_key) DO UPDATE SET
  name = EXCLUDED.name,
  description = EXCLUDED.description,
  markdown_content = EXCLUDED.markdown_content,
  resources = EXCLUDED.resources,
  is_enabled = EXCLUDED.is_enabled;
